WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
A tiny San Francisco running live buses, planes, and robotaxies right in your browser is making people dream of a full world version. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 236. Up next are a deep dive on language models and math, cool stuff on a ChatGPT app change and a judge's experiment, and quick bits. Developer Ari Dutil posted on X a working city that needs no download and no gaming computer. It is free, needs no sign up, and runs in the browser already on your phone or lap.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.960
busses follow actual routes and real-world timing, so a stop at a corner feel

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.700
surprisingly natural on a small screen. Boats cross the water and weather shifts

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.340
the lighting and atmosphere so the streets feel alive rather than stuck on a

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:48.080
loop. You can hop into a robotaxi, a self-driving taxi, and ride the miniature

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.320
streets yourself. Treat it like a digital model train set whose vehicles keep

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.140
real city schedules and update live instead of repeating a fixed show. Open the

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.700
This small SF website on your phone or laptop, click around the map and ride a robot

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.800
taxi during a study break or a commute.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.760
People are already talking about scaling the same idea to the whole planet, so students

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.220
could watch geography, climate, and traffic without leaving their desks or buying expensive

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:13.980
software.

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:20.419
Language models, a kind of artificial intelligence or AI that guessed the next word, just got

00:01:20.419 --> 00:01:21.339
much better at math.

00:01:21.919 --> 00:01:25.359
Your phone keyboard already guesses the next word while you text a friend.

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:29.819
Stretch that guessing game across an entire math problem instead of one sentence.

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:33.619
First, the model looks at the numbers and symbols already on the page.

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:37.259
Next, it predicts the step most likely to make the whole answer correct.

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:43.419
A few years ago, those guesses were often wrong because training, the pile of examples the model studied,

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:45.779
did not contain enough worked out solutions.

00:01:46.419 --> 00:01:52.879
Recently, that pile of solved math problems grew huge, so the guesses started landing on the right answers far more often.

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:56.159
The model is not understanding math the way a person does.

00:01:56.739 --> 00:02:00.719
It is spotting patterns and completing a very long pattern it memorized at scale.

00:02:01.379 --> 00:02:05.179
Accuracy on new problems rose sharply even though the methods stayed the same.

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:10.679
Talk shifted in the past year or two toward problems that once seemed years away, including

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:15.219
advanced questions like the Riemann hypothesis, because the old assumption they would stay

00:02:15.219 --> 00:02:17.319
weak no longer matches what they can do.

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:22.119
you. Once you see the engine as next step guessing fed by a much bigger stack of worked

00:02:22.119 --> 00:02:28.691
examples, those sudden math headlines click into place. Open AI added a new dots tab to

00:02:28.691 --> 00:02:34.751
chat GPT, its chat bot phone app, and rearrange the bottom menu so chats, settings, and tools

00:02:34.751 --> 00:02:39.851
take fewer tabs. If you already have the app, open it and look for the new tab at the bottom.

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:45.411
Start a fresh conversation, then switch back to older chats with the updated menu, and

00:02:45.411 --> 00:02:48.431
And notice how the bar feels more consistent from screen to screen.

00:02:49.051 --> 00:02:50.611
The core features stayed the same.

00:02:51.111 --> 00:02:55.591
The app is used by millions of people every day, so even a small layout change can save

00:02:55.591 --> 00:02:57.291
time when you jump between tasks.

00:02:58.365 --> 00:03:04.045
Sir Jeffrey Voss, a senior English judge and former master of the roles, asked chat GPT

00:03:04.445 --> 00:03:07.865
in log-as-et coverage how to protect people from digital fakes.

00:03:08.245 --> 00:03:13.465
He received a full essay plus three new legal clauses, and he used that reply while preparing

00:03:13.465 --> 00:03:16.045
his Mayflower lecture at the University of Plymouth.

00:03:16.605 --> 00:03:21.585
He has spoken often about new technologies, and he asked specifically how Article 8,

00:03:22.045 --> 00:03:26.485
the privacy right in the European Convention on Human Rights, should be amended to guard

00:03:26.485 --> 00:03:31.285
against cyber fakes, meaning realistic digital copies of a person that were never real.

00:03:31.805 --> 00:03:34.085
The reply drafted three extra sub clauses.

00:03:34.785 --> 00:03:39.625
One suggested clause would give everyone protection against unauthorized creation or manipulation

00:03:39.625 --> 00:03:43.025
of their identity, likeness or voice by digital means.

00:03:43.645 --> 00:03:46.585
That protection would apply when it causes substantial harm.

00:03:47.145 --> 00:03:51.625
The response also discussed balancing this new protection against article 10 rights on

00:03:51.625 --> 00:03:52.665
freedom of expression.

00:03:53.285 --> 00:03:58.585
Voss himself disagreed with the need for amendments, saying article 8 may already be brought enough

00:03:58.585 --> 00:04:03.625
to cover digital identity issues, and that courts should work out how the existing right applies

00:04:03.625 --> 00:04:04.665
in the age of AI.

00:04:05.225 --> 00:04:08.165
You can try a version of that experiment without being a judge.

00:04:08.165 --> 00:04:14.005
Open chat GPT and type, how would you update the right to privacy for the age of deepfakes?

00:04:14.485 --> 00:04:18.985
Read the answer, then decide which parts feel useful and which parts you disagree with,

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:22.005
including where a human expert might still push back.

00:04:22.385 --> 00:04:27.665
North Korea released its first public AI chatbot, called Spring of Wisdom, with an interface

00:04:27.665 --> 00:04:31.065
meant to resemble the familiar chat layout many people already know.

00:04:32.008 --> 00:04:36.628
Researchers at the Center for Countering Digital Harm found in a mirror report that

00:04:36.628 --> 00:04:42.108
But teen chat GPT accounts for ages 13 to 17 can still surface harmful content.

00:04:42.628 --> 00:04:47.568
Pretending to be teens with homework style questions, they sometimes got advice on self-harm

00:04:47.568 --> 00:04:48.428
or eating disorders.

00:04:49.128 --> 00:04:56.148
41% of direct queries received harmful responses, rising to 82% when requests were framed as homework,

00:04:56.508 --> 00:05:01.248
research, or fiction writing, and eating disorders were the highest risk category, with

00:05:01.248 --> 00:05:03.388
half of initial answers flagged as harmful.

00:05:03.388 --> 00:05:08.748
Open AI introduced those team accounts in August after earlier incidents involving young

00:05:08.748 --> 00:05:09.188
users.

00:05:09.688 --> 00:05:14.668
In the United Kingdom, the parental alert triggered only once for self-harm content and

00:05:14.668 --> 00:05:18.088
never for eating disorder or substance abuse queries during the tests.

00:05:18.608 --> 00:05:19.328
And that's a wrap.

